1. saying “sorry to bother you” before asking a completely normal question
You’re not bothering anyone. But you are teaching them that you think you are.
2. laughing nervously after saying something serious
It makes your own point sound less solid before anyone else even responds.
3. over-explaining a decision no one questioned
The more words you use to justify it, the less certain you sound.
4. immediately offering alternatives when someone says no
One no is enough. You do not need to rescue the moment.
5. checking your phone the second a conversation feels awkward
It reads like you’d rather be anywhere else. Presence carries more weight than people realize.
6. telling people how exhausted, stressed, or busy you are all the time
Calm people talk about what they’re building. Frazzled people narrate how overwhelmed they feel.
7. shrinking your posture when you walk into a room
Your body speaks before you do. Walk in like you were expected.
